§§ `Part 2 -- limbs`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to life` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to life` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 16: the right to life`
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to life` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to life` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 16: the right to life`, `s 16: arbitrarily deprived of life`

#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection from cruel treatment` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection from cruel treatment` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 17(b): treated or punished in a cruel, inhuman or degrading way`
-- treatment with full, free and informed consent: (c) not met
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection from cruel treatment` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection from cruel treatment` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 17(c): subjected to medical or scientific experimentation or treatment without full, free and informed consent`
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection from cruel treatment` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 17(a): subjected to torture`, `s 17(b): treated or punished in a cruel, inhuman or degrading way`, `s 17(c): subjected to medical or scientific experimentation or treatment without full, free and informed consent`

-- an interference neither unlawful nor arbitrary is no limb; a lawful but arbitrary one is
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to privacy` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to privacy` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 25(a): privacy, family, home or correspondence unlawfully or arbitrarily interfered with`
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to privacy` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 25(a): privacy, family, home or correspondence unlawfully or arbitrarily interfered with`
-- (b) needs "unlawfully"; an arbitrary but lawful attack on reputation is not enough
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to privacy` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my right to privacy` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 25(b): reputation unlawfully attacked`

#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection as a child` TRUE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 26(2): a child not given the protection needed, and in the child's best interests, because of being a child`
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection as a child` FALSE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did what happened limit my protection as a child` TRUE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

-- s 29. Arguments: liberty limited, arrested or detained, arbitrary, lawful grounds and procedures, told the reason,
-- proceedings, promptly told, criminal charge, promptly before a court, trial without delay, released, automatic
-- detention, applied to court, decided without delay, found unlawful, ordered release, contract debt.
-- Everything done properly: no limb.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
-- Not arrested at all: (2)-(5) and (7) cannot arise even if the other facts say otherwise.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
-- Held in a watch-house without being told why, arbitrarily and without lawful grounds.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 29(2): arbitrary arrest or detention`, `s 29(3): deprived of liberty other than on grounds, and in accordance with procedures, established by law`, `s 29(4): not informed at the time of arrest or detention of the reason for it`
-- Charged, not promptly brought before a court and not released: (5)(a) and (5)(c).
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 29(5)(a): not promptly brought before a court on a criminal charge`, `s 29(5)(c): not released when paragraph (a) or (b) was not complied with`
-- The same but released: (5)(a) only.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 29(5)(a): not promptly brought before a court on a criminal charge`
-- Trial unreasonably delayed, not released (fork F7 reading of (5)(c)).
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 29(5)(b): not brought to trial without unreasonable delay`, `s 29(5)(c): not released when paragraph (a) or (b) was not complied with`
-- Not charged: (5) does not arise however slow things were.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
-- Proceedings to be brought and not promptly told.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 29(4): not promptly informed about proceedings to be brought`
-- Applied to a court, which found the detention unlawful but neither decided promptly nor ordered release.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 29(7): the court did not decide on the lawfulness of the detention without delay`, `s 29(7): the court did not order release after finding the detention unlawful`
-- Found unlawful and released: no (7) limb.
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Did my arrest or detention limit my right to liberty` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 29(1): the right to liberty and security`, `s 29(6): automatically detained in custody while awaiting trial`, `s 29(8): imprisoned only because of an inability to perform a contractual obligation`

-- s 30. Arguments: deprived of liberty, humane treatment, unconvicted, with convicted, reasonably necessary, treated as
-- unconvicted.
#ASSERT `Was my right to humane treatment limited` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Was my right to humane treatment limited` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 30(1): not treated with humanity and with respect for the inherent dignity of the human person`
-- remand prisoner held with convicted prisoners, not reasonably necessary
#ASSERT `Was my right to humane treatment limited` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 30(2): an unconvicted detainee not segregated from convicted persons, where that was not reasonably necessary`
-- the same, reasonably necessary: no (2) limb
#ASSERT `Was my right to humane treatment limited` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
-- a convicted prisoner held with convicted prisoners: (2) and (3) do not arise
#ASSERT `Was my right to humane treatment limited` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Was my right to humane treatment limited` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 30(1): not treated with humanity and with respect for the inherent dignity of the human person`, `s 30(2): an unconvicted detainee not segregated from convicted persons, where that was not reasonably necessary`, `s 30(3): an unconvicted detainee not treated in a way appropriate for a person who has not been convicted`
#ASSERT `Was my right to humane treatment limited` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

-- s 31. Arguments: charged or a party, competent and impartial, fair, public, exclusion justified, judgment available.
#ASSERT `Was my right to a fair hearing limited` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Was my right to a fair hearing limited` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Was my right to a fair hearing limited` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 31(1): the charge or proceeding not decided by a competent, independent and impartial court or tribunal after a fair and public hearing`
#ASSERT `Was my right to a fair hearing limited` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 31(1): the charge or proceeding not decided by a competent, independent and impartial court or tribunal after a fair and public hearing`, `s 31(3): the judgment or decision not made publicly available`
#ASSERT `Was my right to a fair hearing limited` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

-- s 33. Arguments: a child, accused, uncharged (detained without charge), convicted, detained, with adults, trial quickly,
-- treated for age.
-- A 17-year-old on remand held with adults: (1), with no input for necessity (finding 4).
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 33(1): a detained child not segregated from all detained adults`
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 33(1): a detained child not segregated from all detained adults`, `s 33(2): an accused child not brought to trial as quickly as possible`
-- A child detained without charge and held with adults: (1); (2) reaches only an accused child, so no (2) limb even with
-- trial quickly false.
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 33(1): a detained child not segregated from all detained adults`
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
-- An accused child on bail (not detained) not brought to trial as quickly as possible: (2) only.
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 33(2): an accused child not brought to trial as quickly as possible`
-- A convicted child held with adults: (1) does not reach a convicted child (finding 4); (3) if not treated for age.
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 33(3): a convicted child not treated in a way appropriate for the child's age`
-- An adult: nothing in s 33.
#ASSERT `Were my rights as a child in the criminal process limited` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

-- s 37. Arguments: access limited, discrimination, emergency refused, immediately necessary.
#ASSERT `Was my right to health services limited` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY
#ASSERT `Was my right to health services limited` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS LIST `s 37(1): access to health services refused or limited with discrimination`
#ASSERT `Was my right to health services limited` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS LIST `s 37(2): refused emergency medical treatment immediately necessary to save life or prevent serious impairment`
#ASSERT `Was my right to health services limited` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS EMPTY

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 8, 58`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Was the act compatible with my human rights` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `compatible: it limits no human right (s 8(a))`
#ASSERT `Was the act compatible with my human rights` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `compatible: it limits a human right only to the extent reasonable and demonstrably justifiable under s 13 (s 8(b))`
#ASSERT `Was the act compatible with my human rights` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not compatible: it limits a human right beyond what is reasonable and demonstrably justifiable under s 13 (ss 8, 13)`

`U both` MEANS `unlawful: the public entity acted or decided in a way not compatible with human rights, and in deciding failed to give proper consideration to a relevant human right (s 58(1)(a) and (b))`
`U a` MEANS `unlawful: the public entity acted or decided in a way not compatible with human rights (s 58(1)(a))`
`U b` MEANS `unlawful: in making the decision the public entity failed to give proper consideration to a relevant human right (s 58(1)(b))`
`U not met` MEANS `s 58(1) not met on these facts: the act or decision was compatible with human rights and, for a decision, proper consideration was given`
`U before` MEANS `the Act does not apply: an act or decision of a public entity before the commencement (s 108(2)(b))`
`U private` MEANS `s 58 does not apply: an act or decision of a private nature (s 58(4))`
`U religious` MEANS `s 58(1) does not apply: a body established for a religious purpose, acting in accordance with its doctrine and as necessary to avoid offending the religious sensitivities of its people (s 58(3))`
`U law` MEANS `s 58(1) does not apply: the public entity could not reasonably have acted or decided differently because of a statutory provision or other law (s 58(2))`
`U not entity` MEANS `s 58 does not reach it: s 58(1) speaks only of a public entity, and the body is not shown to be one (s 9)`

-- Arguments after the ten s 9 facts: before commencement, private nature, religious body, no choice under law, right
-- limited, justified, a decision, right relevant, proper consideration.
-- A private prison operator for the State, unjustified limit by an act (not a decision): (a).
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `private` `prison` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `U a`
-- The same operator, not for the State: not a public entity.
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `private` `prison` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `U not entity`
-- A hospital and health service deciding to refuse treatment without considering the right, the limit justified: (b) only.
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `statutory` `health` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `U b`
-- The same, the limit not justified: both.
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `statutory` `health` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `U both`
-- A decision with proper consideration and a justified limit: not met.
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `police` `other fn` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `U not met`
-- A decision where no human right was relevant: (b) cannot arise; no limit: not met.
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `police` `other fn` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `U not met`
-- Before the commencement, private nature, religious body, compelled by law: each beats an unjustified limit.
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `police` `other fn` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `U before`
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `council` `other fn` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `U private`
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `private` `other fn` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `U religious`
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `gov` `other fn` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `U law`
-- A court deciding a case: excluded, so s 58 does not reach it.
#ASSERT `Was the act unlawful under the Act` `tribunal` `other fn` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `U not entity`
#ASSERT `is unlawful under s 58` `U a`
#ASSERT (NOT (`is unlawful under s 58` `U law`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`is unlawful under s 58` `U not met`))

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 59`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can I add a human rights ground to my claim` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: I may seek the same relief or remedy on the ground of unlawfulness under s 58, even if I do not succeed on my other ground (s 59(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Can I add a human rights ground to my claim` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: I may rely on s 58 unlawfulness only under s 59, which needs a claim to relief or remedy on another ground of unlawfulness (s 59(1), (5))`
#ASSERT `Can I add a human rights ground to my claim` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no ground to add: the act or decision is not shown to be unlawful under s 58`
#ASSERT `Can I add a human rights ground to my claim` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I may rely on s 58 unlawfulness only under s 59, which needs a claim to relief or remedy on another ground of unlawfulness (s 59(1), (5))`
#ASSERT `Can I get damages for the unlawfulness` TRUE EQUALS `no damages on the s 58 ground (s 59(3)); the damages my other claim gives are not affected (s 59(6))`
#ASSERT `Can I get damages for the unlawfulness` FALSE EQUALS `no damages on the s 58 ground (s 59(3)), and no other right to damages on these facts`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 45, 48`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`R yes` MEANS `yes: so far as possible consistently with its purpose, the provision must be interpreted compatibly with human rights, or else most compatibly (s 48(1), (2)); it stays valid if it cannot be (s 48(4)), and a declaration of incompatibility gives me no right or cause of action (s 54)`
`R override` MEANS `no: s 48 does not apply to a provision the subject of an override declaration in force (ss 45(1), 48(5))`
`R not statutory` MEANS `no: s 48 applies to statutory provisions, that is Queensland Acts and statutory instruments (schedule 1)`
`R before` MEANS `no: the Act does not affect proceedings commenced or concluded before its commencement (s 108(2)(a))`
`qld act` MEANS `a Queensland Act`
`qld instrument` MEANS `a Queensland statutory instrument (a regulation, rule or other instrument made under a Queensland Act)`

#ASSERT `s 45(2) -- the last day of an override provision` (`d` 1 7 2020) EQUALS `d` 1 7 2025
#ASSERT `s 45(2) -- the last day of an override provision` (`d` 29 2 2024) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2029

-- Arguments: kind of law, proceeding before commencement, override made, override commenced, earlier expiry stated,
-- earlier expiry, day of reading.
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `R yes`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld instrument` FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `R yes`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `the common law (judge-made law, such as the law of negligence)` FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `R not statutory`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `a Commonwealth Act, or an instrument made under one` FALSE FALSE `unused` FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `R not statutory`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` TRUE FALSE `unused` FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 6 2026) EQUALS `R before`
-- Override commenced 1 July 2020: in force on the day it commenced and on 1 July 2025; expired 2 July 2025 (fork F12).
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) FALSE `unused` (`d` 30 6 2020) EQUALS `R yes`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 7 2020) EQUALS `R override`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 7 2025) EQUALS `R override`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) FALSE `unused` (`d` 2 7 2025) EQUALS `R yes`
-- An earlier expiry day of 1 January 2024: in force on 31 December 2023, expired on 1 January 2024.
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) TRUE (`d` 1 1 2024) (`d` 31 12 2023) EQUALS `R override`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) TRUE (`d` 1 1 2024) (`d` 1 1 2024) EQUALS `R yes`
-- An "earlier" day later than the 5 years changes nothing: expired after 1 July 2025 anyway.
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) TRUE (`d` 1 1 2027) (`d` 2 7 2025) EQUALS `R yes`
-- From 29 February 2024: in force on 28 February 2029, expired on 1 March 2029.
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 29 2 2024) FALSE `unused` (`d` 28 2 2029) EQUALS `R override`
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `qld act` FALSE TRUE (`d` 29 2 2024) FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 3 2029) EQUALS `R yes`
-- An override over the common law is meaningless here: not a statutory provision comes first.
#ASSERT `Must the statute be read compatibly with my rights` `the law of another State, a Territory or another country` FALSE TRUE (`d` 1 7 2020) FALSE `unused` (`d` 1 7 2021) EQUALS `R not statutory`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 49`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`QCAT` MEANS `another court or tribunal (for example the Magistrates Court or QCAT)`
`SC` MEANS `the Supreme Court (Trial Division)`

#ASSERT `Can my question be referred to the Supreme Court` `QCAT` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the question may be referred to the Supreme Court, and the court must not decide the matter while it is pending or depart from the answer (s 49(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can my question be referred to the Supreme Court` `SC` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the question may be referred to the Court of Appeal, as the referral is by the Trial Division (s 49(2), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can my question be referred to the Supreme Court` `QCAT` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: a referral needs a question of law about the application of the Act, or of interpreting a statutory provision in accordance with it, arising in the proceeding (s 49(1))`
#ASSERT `Can my question be referred to the Supreme Court` `QCAT` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: a referral needs a party's application for it (s 49(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can my question be referred to the Supreme Court` `the District Court` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a referral needs the court or tribunal to consider the question appropriate for the Supreme Court (s 49(2)(b))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 52`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`N both` MEANS `notice in the approved form to both the Attorney-General and the commission (s 52(1))`
`N commission` MEANS `notice in the approved form to the commission only; none to the Attorney-General, as the State is a party (s 52(1), (2)(a))`
`N AG` MEANS `notice in the approved form to the Attorney-General only; none to the commission, as it is a party (s 52(1), (2)(b))`
`N none parties` MEANS `no notice: both the State and the commission are parties (s 52(2))`
`N none` MEANS `no notice: s 52(1) requires one only for a human rights question in the Supreme, District, Land or Land Appeal Court, or a question referred to the Supreme Court under s 49`

-- Arguments: court, question arises, referred, State a party, commission a party.
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `N both`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `SC` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `N both`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `the Land Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `N both`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `the Land Appeal Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `N both`
-- in QCAT or the Magistrates Court a question alone needs no notice; a referral does
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `QCAT` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `N none`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `QCAT` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `N both`
-- suing the State in the District Court
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `N commission`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `N AG`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `N none parties`
#ASSERT `Must I notify the Attorney-General and the commission` `the District Court` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `N none`

-- Arguments add: AG notified, commission notified.
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: I have given every notice s 52 requires`
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: I have still to give a notice s 52 requires`
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: I have still to give a notice s 52 requires`
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: I have still to give a notice s 52 requires`
-- the State a party: the commission notice alone is enough
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: I have given every notice s 52 requires`
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: I have still to give a notice s 52 requires`
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I have given every notice s 52 requires`
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `the District Court` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: s 52 requires no notice from me`
#ASSERT `Have I given the notices` `QCAT` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: s 52 requires no notice from me`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 63, 64`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Who may complain to the commissioner` `the individual the public entity's alleged contravention was about` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the individual the subject of the alleged contravention (s 64(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Who may complain to the commissioner` `an agent of that individual` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: an agent of the individual (s 64(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Who may complain to the commissioner` `a person the commissioner has authorised in writing to complain for the individual` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: a person authorised in writing by the commissioner, who must be satisfied the individual can not make the complaint (s 64(1)(c), (2))`
#ASSERT `Who may complain to the commissioner` `a person the commissioner has authorised in writing to complain for the individual` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: the commissioner may authorise you in writing only if satisfied the individual can not make the complaint (s 64(2))`
#ASSERT `Who may complain to the commissioner` `someone else (for example a relative or an organisation that is not the individual's agent)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not shown: s 64 does not list you; an agent of the individual, or a person the commissioner authorises, may complain`
#ASSERT `Who may complain to the commissioner` `the individual the public entity's alleged contravention was about` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a human rights complaint is about an alleged contravention of s 58(1) by a public entity (s 63)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 65, s 70(1)(d) -- the dates`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`holidays 2026` MEANS LIST `d` 3 4 2026, `d` 6 4 2026, `d` 4 5 2026

#ASSERT `is a business day` (`d` 2 3 2026) `no holidays`
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a business day` (`d` 7 3 2026) `no holidays`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a business day` (`d` 8 3 2026) `no holidays`))
#ASSERT (NOT (`is a business day` (`d` 3 4 2026) `holidays 2026`))
#ASSERT `is a business day` (`d` 3 4 2026) `no holidays`

#ASSERT `From what day can I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 2 3 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `d` 5 5 2026
#ASSERT `From what day can I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 2 3 2026) `holidays 2026` EQUALS `d` 8 5 2026
#ASSERT `From what day can I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 7 4 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `d` 10 6 2026
#ASSERT `From what day can I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 8 4 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `d` 11 6 2026
#ASSERT `From what day can I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 1 5 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `d` 4 7 2026

`C yes` MEANS `yes: you complained to the public entity, at least 45 business days have passed, and you have had no response or one you consider inadequate (s 65(1))`
`C early` MEANS `yes, early: the commissioner may accept it before the 45 business days have passed, as the commissioner considers it appropriate because of exceptional circumstances (s 65(2))`
`C not yet` MEANS `not yet: 45 business days have not passed since you complained to the public entity (s 65(1)(b)); see the earliest day`
`C first` MEANS `no: you must first complain to the public entity about the alleged contravention (s 65(1)(a))`
`C adequate` MEANS `no: you have had a response from the public entity that you do not consider inadequate (s 65(1)(c))`

-- Arguments: complained to entity, day complained, day of my complaint, holidays, had a response, response
-- inadequate, exceptional circumstances.
-- The 45th business day itself is too early; the next day is in time.
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 4 5 2026) `no holidays` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `C not yet`
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 5 5 2026) `no holidays` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `C yes`
-- With the holidays supplied, 5 May is too early and 8 May is the first day.
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 7 5 2026) `holidays 2026` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `C not yet`
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 8 5 2026) `holidays 2026` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `C yes`
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 4 5 2026) `no holidays` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `C early`
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` FALSE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 1 9 2026) `no holidays` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `C first`
-- An inadequate response does not stop the complaint; an adequate one does, even after the wait.
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 5 5 2026) `no holidays` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `C yes`
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 5 5 2026) `no holidays` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `C adequate`
-- s 65(2) lifts only the wait, not the adequate-response condition.
#ASSERT `Can I complain to the commissioner now` TRUE (`d` 2 3 2026) (`d` 4 5 2026) `no holidays` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `C adequate`

#ASSERT `By when should I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 10 6 2025) EQUALS `d` 10 6 2026
#ASSERT `By when should I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 29 2 2024) EQUALS `d` 28 2 2025
#ASSERT `By when should I complain to the commissioner` (`d` 31 12 2025) EQUALS `d` 31 12 2026

`T in` MEANS `in time: made within 1 year after the alleged contravention, so s 70(1)(d) gives no ground to refuse`
`T late` MEANS `late: the commissioner may refuse to deal with it under s 70(1)(d) (a discretion, not a bar); if refused, it lapses and you can not complain again about it (s 72)`

#ASSERT `Can my complaint be refused as late` (`d` 10 6 2025) (`d` 10 6 2026) EQUALS `T in`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be refused as late` (`d` 10 6 2025) (`d` 11 6 2026) EQUALS `T late`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be refused as late` (`d` 29 2 2024) (`d` 28 2 2025) EQUALS `T in`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be refused as late` (`d` 29 2 2024) (`d` 1 3 2025) EQUALS `T late`

`B yes` MEANS `yes: you can wait the 45 business days and still complain within the year; the window is from the earliest day to the last day`
`B no` MEANS `no: the 45 business days end after the 1-year limit; you would need the commissioner to accept the complaint early for exceptional circumstances (s 65(2)) or not to refuse it as late (s 70(1)(d))`

-- Contravention 10 June 2025, last day 10 June 2026. Complaint to the entity on 7 April 2026: earliest day 10 June 2026,
-- the last day itself. On 8 April: earliest 11 June, one day too late.
#ASSERT `Can I meet both time periods` (`d` 10 6 2025) (`d` 2 3 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `B yes`
#ASSERT `Can I meet both time periods` (`d` 10 6 2025) (`d` 7 4 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `B yes`
#ASSERT `Can I meet both time periods` (`d` 10 6 2025) (`d` 8 4 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `B no`
#ASSERT `Can I meet both time periods` (`d` 10 6 2025) (`d` 1 5 2026) `no holidays` EQUALS `B no`
-- With the holidays supplied (3 and 6 April fall before 7 April; 4 May falls in the span and is not counted), the
-- 7 April complaint's 45th business day is Wednesday 10 June and the earliest day Thursday 11 June: one day too late.
#ASSERT `Can I meet both time periods` (`d` 10 6 2025) (`d` 7 4 2026) `holidays 2026` EQUALS `B no`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 67, 102`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Is my complaint in the required form` TRUE TRUE `an email address` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the complaint is in writing, states your name and an address for service, and has enough detail of the alleged contravention (ss 67(1), 102)`
#ASSERT `Is my complaint in the required form` TRUE TRUE `a post office box address` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the complaint is in writing, states your name and an address for service, and has enough detail of the alleged contravention (ss 67(1), 102)`
#ASSERT `Is my complaint in the required form` FALSE TRUE `an email address` TRUE EQUALS `no: it must be written; the commissioner must give you reasonable help to put it in writing if satisfied you need it (s 67(1)(a), (2))`
#ASSERT `Is my complaint in the required form` TRUE TRUE `none, or another kind` TRUE EQUALS `no: it must state your name and an address for service: a residential or business address, a post office box or an email address (ss 67(1)(b), 102)`
#ASSERT `Is my complaint in the required form` TRUE FALSE `a residential address` TRUE EQUALS `no: it must state your name and an address for service: a residential or business address, a post office box or an email address (ss 67(1)(b), 102)`
#ASSERT `Is my complaint in the required form` TRUE TRUE `a business address` FALSE EQUALS `no: it must include enough details to indicate the alleged contravention (s 67(1)(c))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 71, 72`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`L barred` MEANS `the complaint lapses, and you can not make a further complaint about the same alleged contravention (s 72(1)); you must be given notice of the refusal and the reasons (s 71(1))`
`L again` MEANS `the complaint lapses, but you may complain again about the same contravention once the s 65 requirements are met, as the refusal was under s 70(1)(c) (s 72(1)(a), (2)); you must be given notice of the refusal and the reasons (s 71(1))`
`L alive` MEANS `the complaint does not lapse: s 72 speaks only of refusal; a deferral keeps it alive, and you must be given notice of the deferral and the reasons (s 71(1))`

#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `refused it as frivolous, trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 69)` EQUALS `L barred`
#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `refused it as there is a more appropriate course under another law (s 70(1)(a))` EQUALS `L barred`
#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `refused it as already appropriately dealt with by another entity (s 70(1)(b))` EQUALS `L barred`
#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `refused it as the s 65 requirements were not met (s 70(1)(c))` EQUALS `L again`
#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `refused it as not made within 1 year after the contravention (s 70(1)(d))` EQUALS `L barred`
#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `refused to continue as I did not comply with a reasonable request (s 70(2)(a))` EQUALS `L barred`
#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `refused to continue as I could not be contacted (s 70(2)(c))` EQUALS `L barred`
#ASSERT `What happens if my complaint is refused` `deferred dealing with it (s 70(3))` EQUALS `L alive`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 66, 73, 75`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`to ombudsman` MEANS `the ombudsman, the health ombudsman, the Crime and Corruption Commission, the information commissioner or the NDIS commissioner (s 73)`
`to ADA` MEANS `dealt with by the commission as an Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 complaint (s 75)`
`to HRC` MEANS `from the ombudsman, health ombudsman, Crime and Corruption Commission or information commissioner to the human rights commissioner (s 66)`
`P no consent` MEANS `no: it may be passed on only with your consent (ss 66(2)(b), 73(6)(a), 75(2))`

#ASSERT `Can my complaint be passed to another body` `to ombudsman` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: with your consent, and as the commissioner considers it more appropriately dealt with there (s 73(6)); the commissioner must tell you it has been referred (s 73(7)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be passed to another body` `to ombudsman` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `P no consent`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be passed to another body` `to ombudsman` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the commissioner may refer it only if the commissioner considers it would be more appropriately dealt with there (s 73(6)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be passed to another body` `to ADA` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: with your consent, the commissioner may deal with it under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1991, as made on the day of your human rights complaint (s 75(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be passed to another body` `to ADA` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `P no consent`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be passed to another body` `to HRC` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes: with your consent, the referral entity may refer it to the human rights commissioner (s 66(2)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can my complaint be passed to another body` `to HRC` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `P no consent`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `what the commissioner must give me`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `What must the commissioner give me` `the commissioner refused to deal, or to continue to deal, with my complaint` EQUALS `notice of the refusal and the reasons for it (s 71(1))`
#ASSERT `What must the commissioner give me` `the commissioner deferred dealing with my complaint` EQUALS `notice of the deferral and the reasons for it (s 71(1))`
#ASSERT `What must the commissioner give me` `the commissioner referred my complaint to another entity` EQUALS `a notice stating the complaint has been referred to the entity (s 73(7)(b))`
#ASSERT `What must the commissioner give me` `the commissioner accepted my complaint for resolution` EQUALS `notice of the acceptance, stating the commission's role in trying to resolve it and the commissioner's powers (s 76(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `What must the commissioner give me` `the commission finished dealing with my complaint and it was not resolved` EQUALS `a copy of the commissioner's report on the unresolved complaint, prepared as soon as practicable, with its substance and the actions taken (s 88(2), (3), (5))`
#ASSERT `What must the commissioner give me` `my complaint was resolved` EQUALS `as soon as practicable, a notice stating the outcome and that the commission has finished dealing with the complaint (s 89(2))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 78`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`I must` MEANS `yes: you must comply with the direction within the period stated in the notice; the commissioner may file it with a court and it is then enforceable as a court order (s 78(5), (7), (8))`

#ASSERT `Must I give the commissioner the information` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `I must`
#ASSERT `Must I give the commissioner the information` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: you need not comply, as you have a reasonable excuse, such as legal professional privilege (s 78(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Must I give the commissioner the information` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no duty: a request is not a direction, but if it is a reasonable request and you do not comply the commissioner may refuse to continue with your complaint (s 70(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must I give the commissioner the information` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a notice under s 78 must state the purpose of the request; this one does not (s 78(3))`

-- Notice Monday 2 November 2026, period ending Monday 16 November 2026 (14 days).
#ASSERT `Have I complied with the direction in time` TRUE TRUE FALSE (`d` 2 11 2026) (`d` 16 11 2026) TRUE (`d` 10 11 2026) EQUALS `yes: you gave the information within the period stated in the direction`
#ASSERT `Have I complied with the direction in time` TRUE TRUE FALSE (`d` 2 11 2026) (`d` 16 11 2026) TRUE (`d` 16 11 2026) EQUALS `yes: you gave the information within the period stated in the direction`
#ASSERT `Have I complied with the direction in time` TRUE TRUE FALSE (`d` 2 11 2026) (`d` 16 11 2026) TRUE (`d` 17 11 2026) EQUALS `no: you have not given the information within the period stated in the direction`
#ASSERT `Have I complied with the direction in time` TRUE TRUE FALSE (`d` 2 11 2026) (`d` 16 11 2026) FALSE `unused` EQUALS `no: you have not given the information within the period stated in the direction`
#ASSERT `Have I complied with the direction in time` TRUE TRUE FALSE (`d` 2 11 2026) (`d` 16 11 2026) TRUE (`d` 2 11 2026) EQUALS `yes: you gave the information within the period stated in the direction`
#ASSERT `Have I complied with the direction in time` FALSE TRUE FALSE (`d` 2 11 2026) (`d` 16 11 2026) FALSE `unused` EQUALS `no duty to comply on these facts (see the s 78 question)`
#ASSERT `Have I complied with the direction in time` TRUE TRUE TRUE (`d` 2 11 2026) (`d` 16 11 2026) FALSE `unused` EQUALS `no duty to comply on these facts (see the s 78 question)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 82-84`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Must I attend the conciliation conference in person` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: you must attend in person; with the commissioner's consent you may bring a support person, and you may be helped by an interpreter or another person needed to make it intelligible (ss 82(1), (2), 84)`
#ASSERT `Must I attend the conciliation conference in person` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a child need not attend in person (s 82(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must I attend the conciliation conference in person` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: a person with impaired capacity need not attend in person (s 82(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must I attend the conciliation conference in person` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the commissioner has consented to another person attending for you (s 82(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can I be represented at the conciliation conference` TRUE EQUALS `yes: the commissioner has consented, on any reasonable conditions, which can be withdrawn if they are not complied with (s 83(1), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I be represented at the conciliation conference` FALSE EQUALS `no: you may be represented at a conciliation conference only with the commissioner's consent (s 83(1))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 86, 88(6)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

`conference` MEANS `something said or done in the conciliation conference`
`report` MEANS `the commissioner's report on my unresolved complaint`
`HRA proceeding` MEANS `a proceeding about a contravention of the Human Rights Act`
`other proceeding` MEANS `another civil, criminal or administrative proceeding (for example my claim in negligence)`

#ASSERT `Can the conference or report be used in court` `conference` `other proceeding` FALSE EQUALS `no: nothing said or done in a conciliation conference is admissible in any criminal, civil or administrative proceeding unless you and the respondent agree (s 86)`
#ASSERT `Can the conference or report be used in court` `conference` `HRA proceeding` FALSE EQUALS `no: nothing said or done in a conciliation conference is admissible in any criminal, civil or administrative proceeding unless you and the respondent agree (s 86)`
#ASSERT `Can the conference or report be used in court` `conference` `other proceeding` TRUE EQUALS `yes: both you and the respondent agree (ss 86, 88(6))`
#ASSERT `Can the conference or report be used in court` `report` `HRA proceeding` FALSE EQUALS `no: the report is not admissible in a proceeding about a contravention of the Act unless you and the respondent agree (s 88(6))`
#ASSERT `Can the conference or report be used in court` `report` `other proceeding` FALSE EQUALS `not answered: s 88(6) speaks only of a proceeding about a contravention of this Act; for another proceeding the Act does not say`
#ASSERT `Can the conference or report be used in court` `report` `HRA proceeding` TRUE EQUALS `yes: both you and the respondent agree (ss 86, 88(6))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 87`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Does conciliation affect my right to relief` TRUE EQUALS `no: taking part in the conciliation conference does not affect any right you may have to seek relief or a remedy for a contravention of s 58(1) (s 87); whether you have one is s 59`
#ASSERT `Does conciliation affect my right to relief` FALSE EQUALS `s 87 does not arise: you have not taken part in a conciliation conference`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 90, 99, 100`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can the commissioner publish my information` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not answered: s 90(1) gives the power to publish for a complaint the commission has finished dealing with; for one still open the Act does not say`
#ASSERT `Can the commissioner publish my information` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the commissioner may publish information about the complaint, but not your personal information (s 90(1), (2)(c))`
#ASSERT `Can the commissioner publish my information` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the commissioner may publish information about the complaint, including your personal information, as you have published it or given it for publication before (s 90(1), (2)(c))`

#ASSERT `Am I protected for giving information` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: you are not liable civilly, criminally or under an administrative process for giving it, and are not taken to have breached professional ethics or standards (s 99(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Am I protected for giving information` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 99 gives no protection on these facts; it applies to information given honestly under the Act to the commissioner or another entity about a human rights complaint (s 99(1))`
#ASSERT `Am I protected for giving information` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 99 gives no protection on these facts; it applies to information given honestly under the Act to the commissioner or another entity about a human rights complaint (s 99(1))`

#ASSERT `Have I breached an anonymity direction` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `breach: disclosing the information contrary to the direction is an offence, maximum 35 penalty units for an individual (s 100(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Have I breached an anonymity direction` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no breach: you have complied with the direction by not disclosing the information (s 100(2))`
